      <!-- INTRO -->
      <p><strong>CARB aerosol VOC regulations</strong> are limits set by the California Air Resources Board on the volatile organic compound content of aerosol consumer products sold in California, expressed as a maximum weight percent by product category. They work by capping the share of a formulation — including propellant — that can consist of reactive VOCs, forcing manufacturers to select lower-reactivity solvents, alternative propellants, or reformulated actives. This matters for aerosol manufacturers because California's limits are frequently the strictest in the US market and are widely adopted by other states, meaning CARB compliance often functions as the de facto national reformulation target.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 1 -->
      <section id="section-1">
        <h2>What Is the CARB Consumer Products Regulation?</h2>
        <p>The California Air Resources Board regulates VOC emissions from consumer products, including aerosols, under its Consumer Products Regulation, a framework built to reduce ground-level ozone precursor emissions across California's air basins. VOCs react with nitrogen oxides in the presence of sunlight to form ozone, and consumer products — cleaners, personal care aerosols, automotive products, and coatings among them — have been documented as a meaningful, non-mobile source of VOC emissions in state air quality inventories. CARB's authority to regulate stricter than federal standards flows from a specific waiver under the federal Clean Air Act, a legal basis distinct from EPA's own national aerosol coatings VOC program.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Regulating body:</strong> California Air Resources Board (CARB)</li>
          <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> Clean Air Act waiver permitting California-specific standards</li>
          <li><strong>Scope:</strong> Consumer products sold, supplied, offered for sale, or manufactured for use in California</li>
          <li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Category-specific maximum VOC content limits, expressed as weight percent</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Because California represents one of the largest single consumer product markets in the country, and because a number of other states adopt CARB limits through Ozone Transport Commission model rules, the practical reach of the regulation extends well beyond California's borders. Any manufacturer distributing nationally needs a working understanding of the <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-can-safety-explained/" target="_top">regulatory and safety landscape</a> that CARB sits within before finalizing a formulation intended for broad US distribution.</p>
      </section>

