      <!-- SECTION 2 -->
      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>VOC Limits by Aerosol Product Category</h2>
        <p>CARB's Consumer Products Regulation does not apply a single VOC limit across all aerosol products — it defines dozens of individual product categories, each with its own limit and formal category definition, reflecting differences in typical formulation chemistry, function, and usage volume. Correctly classifying a product into its CARB-defined category is the essential first step in any compliance assessment, since an air freshener, a multi-purpose lubricant, and a hairspray are each measured against a different ceiling. The table below illustrates representative category types; exact current numeric limits must always be confirmed against the live CARB regulation text, since limits are periodically tightened.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Category Type</th>
                <th>Typical Formulation Chemistry</th>
                <th>Regulatory Consideration</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Aerosol air fresheners</td><td>Fragrance oils, solvent carriers, hydrocarbon or compressed gas propellant</td><td>Among the most tightly limited categories due to high sales volume</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Personal care aerosols (hairspray, deodorant)</td><td>Polymers, actives, alcohol or hydrocarbon carrier systems</td><td>Distinct sub-limits for hairspray vs. general personal care</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Multi-purpose lubricants</td><td>Base oils, penetrants, hydrocarbon propellant</td><td>Higher typical limit reflecting functional solvent requirements</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Aerosol coatings / spray paints</td><td>Resins, pigments, solvent blends</td><td>Regulated under a related but separate Aerosol Coatings Regulation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Disinfectants and cleaners</td><td>Actives, surfactants, aqueous or solvent carriers</td><td>Often subject to dual EPA/CARB oversight where pesticidal claims apply</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Formulators entering a new aerosol product category should treat category definition as a compliance question in its own right, not an assumption — CARB's category definitions are based on labeled use and marketed function, and misclassifying a product does not exempt it from the correct limit. For manufacturers building a new product line, our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide/" target="_top">Aerosol Manufacturing 101 guide</a> covers how formulation and regulatory classification decisions interact from the earliest development stage.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3 -->
      <section id="section-3">
        <h2>How VOC Content Is Calculated</h2>
        <p>VOC content under the CARB regulation is generally calculated as the weight percent of the total finished product — concentrate plus propellant — that consists of compounds meeting the regulatory definition of a volatile organic compound. This is a critical distinction for aerosols specifically: unlike a pump-spray or trigger-spray liquid product, an aerosol's propellant is part of the calculation, and hydrocarbon propellants such as isobutane, propane, and butane are themselves classified as VOCs. A formulation that would pass as a non-aerosol liquid product can therefore require different treatment once packaged as an aerosol, because propellant weight is often a substantial fraction of total fill weight.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          Because propellant counts toward the VOC calculation, propellant selection is frequently the single largest lever available for aerosol VOC reformulation — often larger than any single change to the liquid concentrate itself.
        </div>
        <h3>CARB-Exempt Compounds</h3>
        <p>CARB maintains a specific list of compounds exempted from the VOC definition on the basis that they have negligible photochemical reactivity in ozone formation, even though they may be organic and volatile in a general chemical sense. Formulators sometimes substitute a portion of a reactive solvent with a CARB-exempt compound to lower the calculated VOC percentage without removing solvent function entirely, but this exemption list is a living regulatory document, not a fixed reference, and must be checked against the current version before being relied upon in a compliance calculation.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section id="section-4">
        <h2>Reformulation Strategies for CARB Compliance</h2>
        <p>Bringing an aerosol formulation under its CARB category limit typically requires working across three variables together — propellant system, solvent chemistry, and exempt-compound substitution — rather than adjusting a single ingredient in isolation. Propellant substitution toward compressed gases such as nitrogen or carbon dioxide removes a hydrocarbon VOC contributor entirely, though it changes spray characteristics and can require valve and actuator redesign, a trade-off covered in depth in our guide to <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/compressed-vs-liquefied-gas-aerosols-pressure-profiles/" target="_top">compressed gas vs liquefied gas aerosol systems</a>. Partial hydrocarbon-to-compressed-gas hybrid systems are also used where full replacement is not functionally viable.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Propellant substitution:</strong> Replacing or partially replacing hydrocarbon propellant with compressed gas (N₂, CO₂)</li>
          <li><strong>Solvent substitution:</strong> Replacing reactive solvents with CARB-exempt low-reactivity compounds where chemically compatible</li>
          <li><strong>Active loading reduction:</strong> Reformulating actives or carrier ratios to reduce total solvent demand per unit performance</li>
          <li><strong>Water-based reformulation:</strong> Shifting a portion of the formulation to an aqueous base where product performance allows</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Any propellant or solvent substitution made purely to hit a VOC compliance number must still be validated for spray performance, can pressure, material compatibility, and stability — a formulation cannot be considered complete until it passes the same physical and stability testing as the original, not just the VOC calculation.
        </div>
        <p>Reformulation decisions also interact directly with a product's flammability classification, since reducing hydrocarbon propellant content can shift a product's flash point and DOT/CARB flammability category. Our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/flash-point-explained/" target="_top">flash point guide</a> explains how these thresholds are determined and why they should be reassessed after any propellant-driven VOC reformulation, not assumed unchanged.</p>
      </section>

