      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="section-5">
        <h2>CARB vs Federal EPA and Other State VOC Rules</h2>
        <p>CARB regulations operate alongside, not in place of, federal VOC oversight, and the two frameworks are not interchangeable for compliance purposes. The <a href="https://www.epa.gov/stationary-sources-air-pollution/aerosol-coatings" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">US EPA's national Aerosol Coatings VOC program</a> establishes a federal floor for spray paint and coating products specifically, while CARB's broader Consumer Products Regulation covers a much wider range of aerosol categories and, for most of them, sets a stricter limit than any federal equivalent. A product that satisfies the EPA national standard is not automatically compliant in California.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Federal EPA:</strong> Sets national VOC limits primarily for aerosol coatings under 40 CFR Part 59</li>
          <li><strong>CARB:</strong> Sets California-specific limits across a far broader range of consumer product categories, generally stricter</li>
          <li><strong>OTC states:</strong> Multiple Northeastern and other US states adopt CARB limits directly via Ozone Transport Commission model rules</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Because a growing number of states outside California apply CARB's own limits through OTC adoption, many manufacturers treat CARB compliance as the practical national reformulation baseline rather than a California-only requirement. Consult the <a href="https://ww2.arb.ca.gov/our-work/programs/aerosol-coatings-regulation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CARB Aerosol Coatings Regulation program page</a> directly for the current text governing spray paint and coating VOC limits, since this sits as a related but separate rule from the general Consumer Products Regulation.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="section-6">
        <h2>Compliance Documentation &amp; Enforcement</h2>
        <p>Demonstrating CARB compliance requires more than a passing formulation — manufacturers must be able to produce calculation records showing how the declared VOC content was determined, including raw material VOC data for every component and the finished product's propellant fraction. This documentation typically lives alongside a product's safety data sheet and technical data sheet, and should be retained and kept current whenever a formulation changes, since even a supplier-side raw material change can shift calculated VOC content without any deliberate reformulation decision on the manufacturer's part.</p>
        <p>Selling a noncompliant aerosol product in California carries real enforcement exposure — civil penalties, mandatory reformulation, distribution restriction, or recall are all within CARB's enforcement toolkit, and because OTC states mirror CARB limits, a single noncompliant SKU can create liability across a multi-state footprint rather than California alone. Teams moving a formulation from lab-scale development into commercial production should build VOC compliance verification into the standard release checklist described in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/from-lab-to-commercial-launch/" target="_top">lab-to-commercial launch guide</a>, rather than treating it as a late-stage check performed only before the first California shipment. For a wider view of how VOC rules compare across regions beyond the US, see our companion guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/global-aerosol-voc-regulations-comparison/" target="_top">global aerosol VOC regulations</a>.</p>
      </section>

