          <div class="faq-answer">VOC content is generally calculated as the weight percent of the total product formulation that is comprised of compounds meeting the regulatory definition of a volatile organic compound, excluding compounds CARB has specifically exempted (such as certain low-reactivity solvents). For aerosol products, this includes both the liquid concentrate and the propellant, since compressed hydrocarbon propellants such as isobutane and propane are themselves VOCs under the regulation.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Which propellants help reduce VOC content in a CARB-compliant aerosol?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Compressed gases such as nitrogen and carbon dioxide are not classified as VOCs and can significantly reduce a product's calculated VOC percentage compared to a hydrocarbon (LPG) propellant system, since propellant typically makes up a large share of total aerosol formulation weight. Some reformulations also reduce hydrocarbon propellant load or substitute a portion of it with compressed gas in a hybrid system to bring VOC content under a category limit without eliminating hydrocarbon propellant entirely.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Do exempt solvents count toward the VOC limit?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">No. CARB maintains a list of compounds specifically exempted from the VOC definition because they have been determined to have negligible photochemical reactivity in forming ground-level ozone. Formulators sometimes substitute a portion of a reactive solvent with a CARB-exempt compound to reduce calculated VOC content, but exempt-solvent substitution must be verified against the current exemption list, since it is updated periodically and is not a static reference.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What happens if an aerosol product exceeds its CARB VOC limit?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Selling a noncompliant aerosol consumer product in California can result in civil penalties, mandatory reformulation or product recall, and distribution restrictions, since CARB enforcement applies at the point of sale as well as to manufacturers and distributors. Because many other states adopt CARB limits through OTC model rules, a single noncompliant formulation can create exposure across a multi-state market rather than California alone.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does CARB regulate all aerosol product categories the same way?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">No. CARB's Consumer Products Regulation sets VOC limits on a per-category basis — air fresheners, personal care aerosols, multi-purpose lubricants, and aerosol coatings each have their own specific limit and definition, reflecting differences in typical formulation chemistry and usage volume. A formulator must first correctly classify the product into its CARB-defined category before determining which limit applies.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How often do CARB VOC limits change?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">CARB periodically reviews and tightens VOC limits for consumer product categories as part of its ongoing State Implementation Plan obligations to reduce ozone precursor emissions. Limits are not fixed indefinitely, and manufacturers selling into California should treat the current CARB Consumer Products Regulation text as the authoritative source rather than relying on limits cited in older technical literature.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is reformulating for CARB compliance the same as reformulating for EU VOC rules?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Not necessarily. CARB VOC limits, EU aerosol dispenser labeling requirements, and other regional VOC frameworks use different category definitions, calculation methods, and exemption lists, so a formulation compliant in one jurisdiction is not automatically compliant in another. Multi-market manufacturers typically need to verify VOC compliance separately against each target jurisdiction's specific regulatory text rather than assuming equivalence.</div>
        </details>
